<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Golden Ambrosian Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1447 CE – 1450 CE · capital Milan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Milanese republic proclaimed when the Visconti line ended in 1447, which fell to its own general Francesco Sforza.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-maria-visconti">Filippo Maria Visconti</a> died without a male heir in August 1447, a group of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Milanese</a> nobles and lawyers proclaimed a republic named after the city's patron saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a>. Many subject cities of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> seized the chance to break away.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was led by elected captains and defenders of liberty, but split between nobles and popular leaders. Threatened by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice</a>, it hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, husband of the late duke's illegitimate daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bianca-maria-visconti">Bianca Maria</a>, who defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venetians</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caravaggio">Caravaggio</a> in 1448. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">Sforza</a> then made a separate peace with Venice and turned against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Milan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">Sforza</a> besieged the city, and in February 1450, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Milan</a> starving, the citizens opened the gates. He was acclaimed duke, restoring the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> under the Sforza dynasty.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/golden-ambrosian-republic.jpg" alt="Golden Ambrosian Republic" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Facquis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Golden_Ambrosian_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1447 CE – 1450 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Milan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lombard, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ambrosian Republic, Aurea Repubblica Ambrosiana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1448&amp;focus=golden-ambrosian-republic" aria-label="Golden Ambrosian Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4898 574 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Golden Ambrosian Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5218 830L5218 830"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Milan. <a href="/atlas?year=1448&amp;focus=golden-ambrosian-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
